<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burning bush</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The burning bush is an event recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-exodus">Book of Exodus</a> and in Islamic scripture, in which a bush burns on Mount Horeb without being consumed. In the biblical account an angel of the Lord appears in a flame from the bush, and God calls to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, who was grazing Jethro's flocks, telling him to take off his sandals because the place is sacred. The voice names itself the God of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a>, Isaac and Jacob and sends Moses to bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. It gives its name as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a>, linked to the phrase &quot;I Am Who I Am&quot;. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> scholars see the account as joined from Yahwist and Elohist texts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burning-bush.jpg" alt="Burning bush" width="330" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Dieric Bouts · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bouts_moses.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
